  "minutes": 22,
  "title": {
    "en": "Marsh and Willow Tit, woodpeckers and treecreepers"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "The last of the lookalikes: tits, woodpeckers and the other treecreeper"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Some pairs have a reputation. Marsh Tit and Willow Tit were not separated until 1897, and many experienced birders still prefer to hear the bird before they commit. The Treecreeper and the Short-toed Treecreeper can't be separated at all without the song or call. This lesson is for those pairs.\n\nThe tits are best separated by the cap (glossy or dull), the wing panel and above all the call. The woodpeckers (Lesser Spotted, Middle Spotted and Black) are the ones that you almost never see, but you can hear: drumming in spring, and calls year-round.\n\nAfter this lesson comes the final exam. You will be able to name the main birds of the whole course, from gulls to waders and from warblers to ducks."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "bib"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The patch of black on the throat and chest. Marsh Tit has a small neat one; Willow Tit has a larger, untidy one."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "cap"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The dark crown of the head. Glossy black in a Marsh Tit, dull sooty in a Willow Tit."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wing panel"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale panel on the folded wing. A Willow Tit often shows a pale one; a Marsh Tit does not."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crest"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A tuft of feathers on the head. The Crested Tit has a pointed, speckled black-and-white one."
      }
    }
  ],
